@namespace AuthWithAdmin.Client.Components

@*
    MStatusBar — the attention marker, EXTRACTED from the mentor Dashboard.

    WHY THIS EXISTS
    The mentor Dashboard and המשימות שלי render the SAME model — a
    MentorAttentionItemDto out of IMentorWorkspaceService — and were drawing
    it two different ways: a 4×40 coloured bar at the reading start of the
    Dashboard's group rows, an 8px MStatusDot at the reading start of the
    Tasks page's rows. Same information, two visual languages, and the
    Dashboard is the source, so the bar is what got named and shared.

    This is a MOVE, not an invention: every value below is
    MentorHomePage.razor.css's own `.mh-attn-bar`, read off
    design/design-system/Motiva Mentor Dashboard.dc.html. Nothing about the
    shape changed on the way out of that stylesheet.

    ── RELATIONSHIP TO MStatusDot ─────────────────────────────────────────
    They answer different questions and both stay.

      MStatusDot   WHICH KIND of thing this row is — a project's signal, a
                   request's lifecycle bucket. The Dashboard uses a dot for
                   exactly that, on הצוותים שבהנחייתי, and so do
                   פרויקטים בהנחייתי and בקשות.

      MStatusBar   HOW URGENTLY this row wants the mentor. It is taller than
                   it is wide on purpose: it reads as a margin rule against a
                   two-line row, which is what an attention queue's rows are.

    So a screen picks the bar when the row is in a "waiting on you" feed and
    the dot when it is in a census. That rule is what keeps one screen from
    growing a second status language again.

    Tone is not open-ended: it resolves to the four semantic roles the
    finished design declares in motiva-tokens.css (`:root .motiva.motiva-final`)
    plus a neutral, so a caller cannot introduce a fifth hue here.
*@

<span class="@RootClass"
      role="@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(AriaLabel) ? null : "img")"
      aria-label="@AriaLabel"
      aria-hidden="@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(AriaLabel) ? "true" : null)"></span>

@code {
    /// <summary>The finished design's four semantic roles, and a neutral for
    /// a row that is in the feed but is not itself a state. Deliberately
    /// closed — see the note above.</summary>
    public enum BarTone { Violet, Blue, Teal, Rose, Neutral }

    [Parameter] public BarTone Tone { get; set; } = BarTone.Blue;

    /// <summary>Accessible name, for the rare case where the bar is the only
    /// carrier of the state. Every current caller states the escalation in
    /// the row's own aria-label instead and leaves this null, which is why
    /// the bar is aria-hidden by default.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? AriaLabel { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Shorter bar for a single-line row. The default is the
    /// Dashboard's 40px, which is sized to a two-line row.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Compact { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    private string RootClass =>
        $"m-status-bar m-status-bar-{Tone.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()}"
        + (Compact ? " m-status-bar-compact" : "")
        + (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Class) ? "" : $" {Class}");
}
